  "content": "% Introduction section for Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol paper\n\n\\subsection{Background and Motivation}\n\nThe global logistics sector is currently at a critical juncture, facing dual pressures of increasing operational efficiency while simultaneously reducing environmental impact. With logistics activities accounting for approximately 10-15\\% of global carbon emissions \\cite{montreuil2011}, there is an urgent need for innovative approaches that can balance these competing objectives. The concept of Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) has emerged as a promising framework for addressing these challenges by integrating digital intelligence with physical logistics operations \\cite{cpilogistics2023}. CPI extends the foundational ideas of the Physical Internet (PI) by incorporating a cyber layer that enhances coordination and control in logistics networks, enabling more efficient resource utilization and improved decision-making \\cite{sarraj2014}.\n\nWithin the CPI paradigm, routing protocols play a crucial role in determining how goods are transported through the network. Traditional routing approaches in logistics have primarily focused on optimizing either time or cost metrics \\cite{b2brouting2023}, with limited consideration for environmental impact. Recent years have seen the emergence of carbon-aware routing protocols that incorporate emissions as an optimization objective \\cite{carbonaware2023}, reflecting growing environmental concerns and increasingly stringent regulatory frameworks aimed at reducing carbon footprints across supply chains.\n\nHowever, a significant limitation of current carbon-aware routing protocols is their reliance on static emission factors that do not account for real-time traffic conditions. This approach fails to capture the dynamic nature of transportation networks, where traffic conditions significantly impact vehicle emissions. Vehicles in congested traffic experience more frequent acceleration and deceleration cycles, leading to higher fuel consumption and emissions per kilometer traveled. Consequently, routes that appear optimal based on static emission calculations may become highly suboptimal when actual traffic conditions are considered.\n\n\\subsection{Research Problem and Challenges}\n\nThe primary research problem addressed in this paper is the development of a carbon-aware routing protocol that dynamically adapts to changing traffic conditions in CPI networks. This problem presents several interrelated challenges:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Dynamic Emission Calculation}: Accurately estimating carbon emissions under varying traffic conditions requires models that account for the complex relationship between vehicle speed, load, and emission rates.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Real-Time Data Integration}: Incorporating live traffic information into routing decisions demands efficient data collection, processing, and integration mechanisms within the CPI framework.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Multi-Criteria Optimization}: Balancing carbon emissions reduction with delivery time constraints requires sophisticated decision-making algorithms that can effectively weigh competing objectives.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Computational Efficiency}: Dynamic route recalculation in response to changing traffic conditions must be computationally efficient to be practical for real-time applications in large-scale logistics networks.\n    \n    \\item \\textbf{Network Adaptability}: The protocol must function effectively across diverse network topologies and varying traffic patterns, from dense urban environments to long-haul intercity routes.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nThese challenges are particularly significant in the context of CPI networks, which aim to integrate fragmented logistics operations into a cohesive, efficient system. While frameworks proposed by Wu et al. \\cite{cpilogistics2023} and Qu et al. \\cite{b2brouting2023} provide valuable foundations for CPI network routing, they lack the integrated consideration of both carbon metrics and real-time traffic data necessary to address the dynamic nature of emissions in transportation networks.\n\n\\subsection{Research Contributions}\n\nThis paper makes several significant contributions to the field of carbon-aware routing in CPI networks:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item We propose a novel Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol that dynamically integrates real-time traffic information with carbon emission calculations to optimize routing decisions in CPI networks.\n    \n    \\item We develop a comprehensive carbon emission calculation model that accounts for vehicle type, load, speed, and traffic conditions, providing more accurate estimations of environmental impact than static approaches.\n    \n    \\item We introduce a multi-criteria decision framework that effectively balances carbon emissions reduction with delivery time constraints, adapting to both shipment priorities and traffic conditions.\n    \n    \\item We design a two-layer CPI architecture that enhances existing frameworks by incorporating traffic data collection points and emission-aware routing mechanisms at both link and transport layers.\n    \n    \\item We conduct extensive experimental evaluation across various traffic scenarios, demonstrating significant improvements in carbon efficiency compared to both traditional routing approaches and static carbon-aware methods.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nTo our knowledge, this is the first work to explicitly address the integration of real-time traffic information in carbon-aware routing protocols for CPI networks. By developing a routing approach that adapts to changing traffic conditions while prioritizing emissions reduction, we bridge a critical gap in current logistics optimization approaches.\n\n\\subsection{Proposed Approach Overview}\n\nOur proposed Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol is built on a two-layer architecture designed to effectively integrate real-time traffic information with carbon emissions data. The architecture consists of a Link Layer and a Transport Layer working in tandem to optimize routing decisions.\n\nThe Link Layer is responsible for the organization of autonomous logistics areas (ALAs) and their interconnections, incorporating real-time traffic data collection points. It establishes and maintains the physical infrastructure representation, enabling the system to monitor current traffic conditions across the network. The Transport Layer handles routing decisions by processing carbon metrics alongside the traffic information provided by the Link Layer.\n\nThe core of our system is the Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Algorithm, which dynamically balances carbon emissions and travel time considerations while adapting to changing traffic conditions. This algorithm employs a multi-criteria decision framework that combines carbon emissions and travel time objectives, with weights that can be adjusted based on shipment priorities and delivery constraints. The system continuously monitors traffic conditions and recalculates routes when significant changes are detected, ensuring that routing decisions remain optimal as network conditions evolve.\n\nOur approach addresses the limitations of existing carbon-aware routing protocols by:\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Integrating carbon emission factors with real-time traffic data in routing tables\n    \\item Dynamically recalculating optimal routes when significant traffic changes are detected\n    \\item Implementing multi-criteria decision making that balances carbon emissions reduction with delivery time constraints\n    \\item Incorporating traffic prediction models to anticipate congestion and proactively adjust routes\n\\end{itemize}\n\nBy combining these innovations, the proposed protocol aims to achieve lower total carbon emissions compared to both static carbon-aware routing and traffic-adaptive routing under variable traffic conditions.\n\n\\subsection{Paper Structure}\n\nThe remainder of this paper is organized as follows: \n\nSection 2 provides a comprehensive review of related work in traditional CPI routing protocols, carbon-aware routing approaches, and traffic-adaptive methods, highlighting the research gap addressed by this paper.\n\nSection 3 presents the methodology of our approach, including the system architecture, carbon emission calculation model, real-time traffic information integration, and adaptive routing algorithm.\n\nSection 4 describes the experimental design, including the network model, traffic scenarios, and compared routing algorithms used to evaluate our approach.\n\nSection 5 presents the experimental results and analysis, comparing our proposed Carbon-Aware Adaptive Routing Protocol against baseline approaches across various metrics including travel time, distance, carbon emissions, and computational overhead.\n\nSection 6 discusses the implications of our findings, including the trade-offs between emissions and time, computational considerations, and performance across different traffic scenarios.\n\nSection 7 concludes the paper with a summary of contributions and directions for future research in carbon-aware adaptive routing for CPI networks.",
